From 7bdd8674998bdf2ea355a2306a9c9e8b32d33d0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Mocny <mmocny@chromium.org>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:56:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/32] Align chrome.csi() and chrome.loadTimes() with
 web-exposed Performance values

Add WebPerformanceTimings and WebLocalFrame::PerformanceTimings() to
provide web-exposed, TimeClamper-coarsened DOMHighResTimeStamp values
from Performance::timeOrigin(), PerformanceNavigationTiming (NTv2),
Performance::FirstPaintTiming(), and Performance::now() for the legacy
chrome.csi() and chrome.loadTimes() bindings:

- All WebPerformanceTimings fields are double milliseconds
  (navigation_start relative to the Unix epoch, and all other milestones
  relative to navigation_start); LoadTimesBindings converts epoch
  timestamps back to integer-millisecond accuracy.
- chrome.csi().pageT now uses Performance::now() instead of computing
  base::Time::Now() - navigationStart.
- chrome.loadTimes().firstPaintTime now uses the web-exposed
  Performance::FirstPaintTiming() entry instead of the internal
  FirstPaintForMetrics() timestamp.
- Remove unused top-level DomContentLoadedEventEnd() and LoadEventEnd()
  methods from WebPerformanceMetricsForReporting.

Bug: 562971921
